<p></p><p><span>. He recently hired “</span><a href="https:///www.chicagotribune.com/news%3Acriminal-justice%3Act-chicago-violent-gathering-police-investigation-20230420-li5tlrt7wbduxfczh2jr3w66c4-story.html" archive="https:///www.chicagotribune.com/2023%3A04%3A19%3Achicago-police-investigating-officers-response-to-mob-attack-in-loop-after-witness-says-police-declined-to-help.html"
>According to a former de Blasio aide, during the general election campaign in 2013 de Blasio’s team was even convinced that members of his 
police detail were eavesdropping on his private conversations in his city-assigned car. Things got so bad that de Blasio, according to the 
staffer, would step into the street to make sure he was out of earshot of plainclothes officers.</q>”</span></p><p>
<strong>February 11, 2014: </strong><span>New York Post </span><a href="https://nypost.com/2014/02/11/bill-de-blasio-called-cops-to-spare-pastor-pal-from-jail/" rel
>reports</a><span> that de Blasio intervened to get a friend and political ally, Bishop Orlando Findlayter, out of jail early after he was arrested on warrants stemming from an arrest at a protest. 
The report cites a “police source,” and also quotes the NYPD police union spokesperson expressing outrage at the incident. This information 
almost certainly was leaked by elements within the NYPD who were privy to the overnight detaining of Findlayter.</span></p>
